1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:03,850
The Three Days Singapore's
Stock Market Closed

2
00:00:03,850 --> 00:00:06,789
On Monday, the 2nd of December 1985,

3
00:00:06,869 --> 00:00:11,650
the Stock Exchange of Singapore
did not open for trading.

4
00:00:11,650 --> 00:00:15,350
Neither did the Kuala
Lumpur Stock Exchange.

5
00:00:15,350 --> 00:00:18,669
Both stayed shut for
three days — the only time

6
00:00:18,749 --> 00:00:22,075
Singapore's market has
ever closed completely.

7
00:00:22,075 --> 00:00:24,416
What forced it was a single company,

8
00:00:24,496 --> 00:00:28,786
Pan-Electric Industries, a
refrigerator maker that had grown into

9
00:00:28,866 --> 00:00:32,148
a sprawling conglomerate,
and a web of undisclosed

10
00:00:32,228 --> 00:00:35,725
contracts that had been
propping up its share price.

11
00:00:35,725 --> 00:00:38,482
At the centre of it was Tan Koon Swan,

12
00:00:38,562 --> 00:00:42,513
a businessman who three weeks
earlier had been elected

13
00:00:42,593 --> 00:00:46,325
leader of Malaysia's main
Chinese political party.

14
00:00:46,325 --> 00:00:50,578
Pan-Electric began in 1956
as Climate Engineering,

15
00:00:50,658 --> 00:00:55,425
importing French Frimatic
refrigerators into Singapore.

16
00:00:55,425 --> 00:00:59,229
Its founder, Ernest
Kahlenberg, moved into local

17
00:00:59,309 --> 00:01:03,760
assembly under the government's
pioneer-industry scheme,

18
00:01:03,840 --> 00:01:07,968
renamed the company
Pan-Electric Industries in 1961,

19
00:01:08,048 --> 00:01:12,175
and opened a factory at
Kampong Arang Road in 1965.

20
00:01:12,175 --> 00:01:15,441
By 1967 it was billed as the largest

21
00:01:15,521 --> 00:01:18,880
refrigerator plant in Southeast Asia,

22
00:01:18,960 --> 00:01:23,700
and in August 1968 it listed
on the stock exchange.

23
00:01:23,700 --> 00:01:28,454
From the mid-1970s the company
diversified quickly and widely:

24
00:01:28,534 --> 00:01:31,807
marine salvage through
a firm called Selco,

25
00:01:31,887 --> 00:01:36,407
property development through
Vanguard Realty, a 1981 merger

26
00:01:36,487 --> 00:01:40,775
with the electrical-goods
maker Acma, stakes in hotels.

27
00:01:40,775 --> 00:01:43,652
By 1985 Pan-Electric sat at the

28
00:01:43,732 --> 00:01:46,975
head of around sixty subsidiaries.

29
00:01:46,975 --> 00:01:50,692
Peter Tham joined the board in 1982;

30
00:01:50,772 --> 00:01:53,725
Kahlenberg had left by 1983.

31
00:01:53,725 --> 00:01:59,209
Tan Koon Swan was a Malaysian who
had made his fortune in Singapore's

32
00:01:59,289 --> 00:02:04,450
stock market in the 1970s, with
a reputation for a golden touch.

33
00:02:04,450 --> 00:02:09,784
He held his interest in Pan-Electric
through two companies he controlled,

34
00:02:09,864 --> 00:02:13,275
Sigma International and
Grand United Holdings.

35
00:02:13,275 --> 00:02:16,917
In November 1985 he was
elected president of the

36
00:02:16,997 --> 00:02:20,795
Malaysian Chinese Association,
a senior partner in

37
00:02:20,875 --> 00:02:25,525
Malaysia's ruling coalition —
a man with money in Singapore's

38
00:02:25,605 --> 00:02:29,250
markets and a hand now
in Malaysian government.

39
00:02:29,250 --> 00:02:31,837
The way Pan-Electric's share price was

40
00:02:31,917 --> 00:02:34,725
supported was through forward contracts.

41
00:02:34,725 --> 00:02:38,582
A shareholder sells a block of
shares to a broking firm now,

42
00:02:38,662 --> 00:02:43,437
with an agreement to buy them back at
a fixed price several months later —

43
00:02:43,517 --> 00:02:47,650
in effect a bet that the price
will have risen in the meantime.

44
00:02:47,650 --> 00:02:52,000
On its own this was an accepted
piece of market practice.

45
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:56,146
What made Pan-Electric's version
dangerous was that the contracts

46
00:02:56,226 --> 00:03:00,372
were large and, for the most part,
undisclosed, and that when the

47
00:03:00,452 --> 00:03:04,664
market softened through 1985 and
the shares fell rather than rose,

48
00:03:04,744 --> 00:03:09,100
the parties committed to buying
them back were facing heavy losses.

49
00:03:09,100 --> 00:03:12,321
Tan Koon Swan's own forward commitments

50
00:03:12,401 --> 00:03:16,125
came to about 140 million
Singapore dollars.

51
00:03:16,125 --> 00:03:19,822
Across the whole market,
brokers' forward exposure

52
00:03:19,902 --> 00:03:23,448
to Pan-Electric and related
stocks was estimated

53
00:03:23,528 --> 00:03:26,700
at close to 600 million Singapore dollars.

54
00:03:26,700 --> 00:03:31,456
As the shares kept sliding, Tan and
Tham worked to hold the price up,

55
00:03:31,536 --> 00:03:34,960
and Tan moved roughly 29
million Singapore dollars

56
00:03:35,040 --> 00:03:38,825
out of company accounts to
keep the arrangement going.

57
00:03:38,825 --> 00:03:44,229
On the 27th of September 1985 the
company announced a 64 million

58
00:03:44,309 --> 00:03:49,450
Singapore dollars rights issue
meant to steady its finances.

59
00:03:49,450 --> 00:03:54,100
On the 18th of November it
missed a payment on a bank loan.

60
00:03:54,100 --> 00:03:59,364
On the 21st of November it
disclosed borrowings of 453 million

61
00:03:59,444 --> 00:04:04,875
Singapore dollars from thirty-five
banks, most of it unsecured.

62
00:04:04,875 --> 00:04:07,476
The banks stopped lending — not only to

63
00:04:07,556 --> 00:04:10,925
Pan-Electric but to the
broking firms tied to it.

64
00:04:10,925 --> 00:04:15,925
On the 30th of November the
company was placed in receivership.

65
00:04:15,925 --> 00:04:20,069
With Pan-Electric in receivership
and its forward contracts

66
00:04:20,149 --> 00:04:23,800
about to come due, the
danger was a chain reaction.

67
00:04:23,800 --> 00:04:27,373
Around half a dozen broking
firms were heavily exposed,

68
00:04:27,453 --> 00:04:32,089
and because Singapore's and Malaysia's
brokers traded with one another,

69
00:04:32,169 --> 00:04:35,025
one failure could pull
others down with it.

70
00:04:35,025 --> 00:04:37,434
On Monday, the 2nd of December,

71
00:04:37,514 --> 00:04:40,725
the two exchanges suspended all trading.

72
00:04:40,725 --> 00:04:43,700
They stayed closed through Wednesday.

73
00:04:43,700 --> 00:04:47,675
Behind the closed doors,
a rescue was put together.

74
00:04:47,675 --> 00:04:51,741
On the 3rd of December the Stock
Exchange of Singapore asked

75
00:04:51,821 --> 00:04:55,264
the four big local banks
— DBS, OCBC, OUB and UOB —

76
00:04:55,344 --> 00:04:59,824
for a credit line, first reported
at 150 million Singapore dollars

77
00:04:59,904 --> 00:05:02,933
and settled at 180
million Singapore dollars,

78
00:05:03,013 --> 00:05:07,563
to keep the exposed brokers afloat
while the forward contracts were

79
00:05:07,643 --> 00:05:11,650
unwound in an orderly way
rather than through a fire sale.

80
00:05:11,650 --> 00:05:16,142
A lawyer named Chan Sek Keong,
later Singapore's Chief Justice,

81
00:05:16,222 --> 00:05:19,850
helped the Monetary Authority
draft the agreement.

82
00:05:19,850 --> 00:05:23,598
The Finance Minister, Richard
Hu, was clear that the fund

83
00:05:23,678 --> 00:05:27,090
was not there to save brokers
who had been reckless,

84
00:05:27,170 --> 00:05:30,918
only to see that the exchange's
own obligations were met,

85
00:05:30,998 --> 00:05:34,625
and that anything drawn would
be repaid with interest.

86
00:05:34,625 --> 00:05:38,825
Even so, about six broking
firms did not survive.

87
00:05:38,825 --> 00:05:43,275
The market reopened on
Friday, the 5th of December.

88
00:05:43,275 --> 00:05:47,800
Pan-Electric was wound up in October 1986.

89
00:05:47,800 --> 00:05:51,249
Its shares were worthless;
thousands of shareholders

90
00:05:51,329 --> 00:05:54,450
and a long list of
creditors carried the loss.

91
00:05:54,450 --> 00:05:59,600
Tan Koon Swan was
arrested in January 1986.

92
00:05:59,600 --> 00:06:03,072
He pleaded guilty, under
a bargain that cut fifteen

93
00:06:03,152 --> 00:06:07,112
charges down to one, to abetting
criminal breach of trust,

94
00:06:07,192 --> 00:06:10,675
and was sentenced to two
years in jail and a fine.

95
00:06:10,675 --> 00:06:14,520
He resigned the MCA
presidency, and the conviction

96
00:06:14,600 --> 00:06:18,525
ended a political career
only weeks into its peak.

97
00:06:18,525 --> 00:06:23,606
Peter Tham was sentenced to eight
years on dozens of forgery charges;

98
00:06:23,686 --> 00:06:27,875
Pan-Electric's finance director
received a shorter term.

99
00:06:27,875 --> 00:06:31,257
Decades later, some in
Malaysia — and the case's

100
00:06:31,337 --> 00:06:36,306
own lead prosecutor — would argue
that Tan had been wrongly convicted,

101
00:06:36,386 --> 00:06:38,550
a view that remains contested.

102
00:06:38,550 --> 00:06:41,925
The lasting change was to the rules.

103
00:06:41,925 --> 00:06:46,464
The Securities Industry Act of
1986 gave the Monetary Authority

104
00:06:46,544 --> 00:06:50,276
far greater control over
the securities business and

105
00:06:50,356 --> 00:06:54,675
tightened the requirements on
forward contracts, on how much

106
00:06:54,755 --> 00:06:59,300
capital a broker had to hold,
and on what had to be disclosed.

107
00:06:59,300 --> 00:07:02,641
The crisis also exposed how entangled the

108
00:07:02,721 --> 00:07:06,725
Singapore and Kuala Lumpur
exchanges had become.

109
00:07:06,725 --> 00:07:09,884
At the end of 1989 the two of them removed

110
00:07:09,964 --> 00:07:13,125
each other's companies from their boards.

111
00:07:13,125 --> 00:07:17,344
So that Singaporeans could
still trade Malaysian shares,

112
00:07:17,424 --> 00:07:20,108
Singapore opened an over-the-counter

113
00:07:20,188 --> 00:07:23,950
market called CLOB
International in January 1990.

114
00:07:23,950 --> 00:07:27,100
CLOB ran quietly for eight years.

115
00:07:27,100 --> 00:07:32,488
Then, in September 1998, in the
depths of the Asian financial crisis,

116
00:07:32,568 --> 00:07:36,292
Malaysia imposed capital
controls and froze it —

117
00:07:36,372 --> 00:07:42,791
around 4 billion Singapore dollars of
shares belonging to about 172,000 investors,

118
00:07:42,871 --> 00:07:46,675
almost all of them
Singaporean, locked in place.

119
00:07:46,675 --> 00:07:50,456
The freeze lasted more than
a year; the shares were

120
00:07:50,536 --> 00:07:54,625
released in stages under a
deal reached in early 2000.

121
00:07:54,625 --> 00:07:58,110
The untangling of the two
markets that began with

122
00:07:58,190 --> 00:08:02,700
Pan-Electric took fifteen years
and a second crisis to finish.

123
00:08:02,700 --> 00:08:06,173
Where it fits in the bigger
story: the shape of it is

124
00:08:06,253 --> 00:08:10,275
familiar to anyone who has
watched a financial crisis since.

125
00:08:10,275 --> 00:08:14,047
It begins with a scheme that
bends convention without

126
00:08:14,127 --> 00:08:17,609
breaking the law — here,
forward contracts run at

127
00:08:17,689 --> 00:08:21,250
a size and secrecy the
rules had not anticipated.

128
00:08:21,250 --> 00:08:24,901
It runs longer than it should
because the person at the

129
00:08:24,981 --> 00:08:28,835
centre is trusted, and large
positions held in a respected

130
00:08:28,915 --> 00:08:32,850
name draw fewer questions than
they would from a stranger.

131
00:08:32,850 --> 00:08:36,306
Then something unforeseen —
a soft market, this time —

132
00:08:36,386 --> 00:08:41,741
turns the bet the wrong way, and what was
one company's problem becomes everyone's,

133
00:08:41,821 --> 00:08:44,557
carried outward along
the web of people who

134
00:08:44,637 --> 00:08:48,500
had lent it money or stood on
the other side of its trades.

135
00:08:48,500 --> 00:08:51,208
Pan-Electric was the first time that

136
00:08:51,288 --> 00:08:54,075
pattern closed a Singapore exchange.

137
00:08:54,075 --> 00:08:57,575
It was not the last time
the pattern would run.
